\description{
Report coefficients, prespecified linear contrasts and residual variance from
an estimated RSM/PCM MML population model. Coefficient intervals use a normal
approximation; residual
variance intervals exponentiate a normal interval for log residual variance.
For these experimental intervals, nominal repeated-sample coverage has not
been established across the supported designs. Passing numerical checks
does not establish this statistical property.
Saved cautions are also retained as an \code{interpretation_notes} table in
\code{\link[=mfrm_results]{mfrm_results()}}, reports and table exports.
}
\details{
For \eqn{\theta_i \mid X_i \sim N(X_i\beta, \sigma_e^2)}, extracts the
inverse full joint observed information (including structural nuisance
parameters) to form normal intervals on working coordinates (\code{beta} and, when
estimated, \verb{\\log\\sigma_e^2} in \code{WorkingEstimate}, \code{WorkingSE},
\code{WorkingLower}, \code{WorkingUpper}, exponentiated to natural variance with
\code{NaturalScaleStatus}) plus optional linear contrasts \eqn{L\widehat\beta} with
covariance \eqn{L V_\beta L^{\mathsf T}} (\code{contrast_covariance}). Explicit
fixed \code{population_sd} retains \code{Status = "fixed"} and \code{CIEligible = FALSE}.

Requires direct RSM/PCM MML (unit weights, full-rank coding, centered
additive facets/steps, passing local/information/finer-quadrature and
zero-residual-variance likelihood checks). For intercept-only anchor-origin
fits, \code{anchor_covariance} with \code{independent_anchors = TRUE} (or a saved
\code{\link[=mfrm_facet_intervals]{mfrm_facet_intervals()}} object) propagates independent source-anchor
uncertainty (\code{AnchorSourceSE} vs \verb{Conditional*} columns). Intervals remain
experimental (\code{Status}, \code{CIEligible}, \code{InferenceReview}) and do not propagate
calibration uncertainty into \code{\link[=predict_mfrm_units]{predict_mfrm_units()}}.

\code{plot()} method: Plot saved latent-population coefficients or contrasts

Read point estimates and their experimental pointwise intervals from an
existing population analysis, without fitting or recalculating uncertainty.

For \code{plot()}: Rows retain their saved order. Filled circles and lines show numerically
available intervals. Crosses with a \verb{CI unavailable} label retain finite
points without eligible finite bounds. A missing point keeps its labeled row
without a marker; it is never plotted at zero. Titles and explanatory notes
are returned, not drawn. No significance colors or automatic classifications
are added. Plotting does not requalify a saved numerical result.

Coefficients describe the conditional population mean on the fitted native
ability scale. A continuous coefficient is per unit of its fitted predictor;
the intercept depends on predictor zero values and factor coding. Read
interactions, transformations and categorical comparisons using the saved
coding, not an assumed reference group. Coefficients on different predictor
scales are not directly comparable measures of importance. Higher ability
predicts higher coded ratings in these RSM/PCM models; whether that is better
performance depends on the rubric. Coefficients are not standardized effects.

Contrasts use the stored linear weights and may describe a conditional mean
or a difference between means. They are not individual ability differences,
causal effects, simultaneous intervals or prediction intervals. Numerical
availability does not establish nominal coverage: the source's experimental
status, failed checks and qualifications remain in \code{notes} and the tables.
A wide interval remains visible. Missing bounds are not infinite endpoints.
When source-anchor uncertainty was requested, the saved intervals include
that contribution. The returned subtitle, settings and \code{anchor_uncertainty}
distinguish it from intervals conditional on exact supplied anchors;
conditional bounds remain in the returned table.

For an executable bundled-data example, run the example on
